Do not look for a POSIX host compiler on Windows.

common.gypi picks host_cc/host_cxx like this:

  ['host_clang==1', { 'host_cc': '<(make_clang_dir)/bin/clang', ... },
                    { 'host_cc': '<!(which gcc)', ... }]

Upstream never reaches the else branch on Windows, because host_clang defaults
to 1 there. We set host_clang=0 (see tools/gyp_sawbuck.py -- we build with MSVC
and do not want the CR_CLANG_REVISION lookup into tools/clang, which DEPS does
not fetch), so we land in it, and GYP shells out to 'which gcc'. On a machine
with no gcc on PATH that returns non-zero and generation fails outright:

  gyp: Call to 'which gcc' returned exit status 1 while in sawbuck/sawbuck.gyp

host_cc and host_cxx are not used by any target Sawbuck builds; they exist for
cross-compiles that need a host toolchain. So on Windows they are set empty,
and the original lookup is kept for every other host.

--- a/build/common.gypi
+++ b/build/common.gypi
@@ -2444,8 +2444,21 @@
         'host_cc': '<(make_clang_dir)/bin/clang',
         'host_cxx': '<(make_clang_dir)/bin/clang++',
       }, {
-        'host_cc': '<!(which gcc)',
-        'host_cxx': '<!(which g++)',
+        'conditions': [
+          ['OS=="win"', {
+            # PATCHED: there is no POSIX host compiler on Windows, and
+            # host_cc/host_cxx are not used by anything we build. Upstream
+            # never reaches this branch because host_clang defaults to 1; we
+            # set it to 0 (see tools/gyp_sawbuck.py), so without this GYP
+            # shells out to 'which gcc' and generation fails outright on any
+            # machine that has no gcc on PATH.
+            'host_cc': '',
+            'host_cxx': '',
+          }, {
+            'host_cc': '<!(which gcc)',
+            'host_cxx': '<!(which g++)',
+          }],
+        ],
       }],
 
       # The seccomp-bpf sandbox is only supported on five architectures
